            <ArticleTitle>Current evidence for the use of sugammadex in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>118-125</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/pan.13050</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Sugammadex is a novel pharmacologic agent, which reverses neuromuscular blockade with a mechanism that differs from the commonly used, acetylcholinesterase inhibitors. Although sugammadex has not received approval from the United States Food &amp; Drug administration in children, its use has already been reported to reverse neuromuscular blockade in several clinical scenarios in the pediatric population including the 'cannot intubate-cannot ventilate' scenario. To date, there remains limited data from prospective trials in the pediatric-aged patient. Anecdotal use has been reported for the reversal of neuromuscular blockade in difficult clinical scenarios such as children with neuromuscular diseases including myasthenia gravis, Duchenne muscular dystrophy, and myotonic dystrophy.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2016 John Wiley &amp; Sons Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
